:- module(polymorphic_constraint_cross_occurrence, [leq/2, result/1]).
:- use_module(library(prelude)).

% Regression: a transitivity rule over a polymorphic constraint must
% type-check. Each head occurrence of leq(T, T) is an implementation
% site and allocates its OWN rigid T — the store may hold an
% leq(1, 2) and an leq(a, b) side by side, so nothing lets one
% occurrence assume the other's instance. What ties them here is the
% shared Y: HNF renames it to leq(X, Y), leq(Y1, Z) plus the
% synthetic GuardEqual(Y, Y1), which is an evidence form and merges
% the two skolems. The body tell leq(X, Z) is a use site, and its
% fresh flexible σ instantiates to the merged T from both arguments.
% See §"Rigid and flexible type variables" and the multi-head worked
% example in §"Guard-Derived Type Evidence" of the type-system spec;
% the negative twin is polymorphic_constraint_unshared.
:- chr_constraint leq(T, T), result(any).

trans @ leq(X, Y), leq(Y, Z) ==> leq(X, Z).

result(R) <=> leq(1, 2), leq(2, 3), R = 0.
